https://drmemory.org/page_download.html

Run Dr. Memory:

You can drag and drop your executable onto the Dr. Memory desktop icon.
Alternatively, you can run it from the command line:
bash
Copier le code
drmemory -- your_program.exe
Dr. Memory will run your program and generate a report detailing any memory issues.










I use  AddressSanitizer (ASan) 

add -fsanitize=address   in complileropriton  and -lasan in liker option
BUt beofr I need to install (unsin Msys2) 
I got proble with gdb so I usenstall Clang:

Install Clang via MSYS2:

bash
Copier le code
pacman -S mingw-w64-x86_64-clang
Configure Code::Blocks to Use Clang:

Open Code::Blocks and go to Settings > Compiler.
Create a new compiler profile and set it to use clang++ for the compiler and linker.
C Compiler: clang.exe
C++ Compiler: clang++.exe
Linker for Dynamic Libs: clang++.exe
Linker for Static Libs: llvm-ar.exe (or ar.exe if you don't have llvm-ar.exe)
Debugger: This can remain as gdb.exe unless you want to switch to LLDB, which is the debugger typically used with Clang/LLVM.
Resource Compiler: Leave as windres.exe (since it's a MinGW tool).




Enable AddressSanitizer:

In your project’s build options, add -fsanitize=address to the compiler flags under Other options.
Also, add -g to include debugging symbols.
Compile and Run Your Program:

Build your project in Code::Blocks. If AddressSanitizer is supported, you can run your program and catch memory errors directly within Code::Blocks.



